  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 329 AM EDT Thu Jul 2 2026\n\nUpper ridging centered over the Mid Atlantic region moves offshore \nthis weekend with a more disorganized pattern taking hold Sunday \ninto next week. Upper level flow will be more quasi-zonal and with \nno strong forcing, a more summer-like pattern reemerges. Hot \ntemperatures/heat indices remain in the forecast with widespread \nhighs in the mid and upper 90s Friday and Independence Day (except \nin the North Georgia mountains where the upper 80s will be more \ncommon). Temperatures trend down slightly Sunday into next week but \nstill remain hot across the area. Heat index values also remain \nelevated through the period even with a slight decrease in \ntemperatures. This is due to increasing moisture returning to the \narea bringing back some higher dewpoints. Additional heat products \nfor some locations cannot be ruled out heading into the holiday \nweekend.\n\nRain chances remain limited Friday and Saturday to the northern or \nwestern portions of the state, but become more widespread Sunday \ninto early next week. Pulse-type convection is expected to continue \nin the afternoons and evenings with diurnal heating each day. A few \nstrong to marginally severe storms cannot be ruled out into next \nweek.",
